**User Stories**
1. AI Academic Navigator
- As a busy student, I want to ask the AI assistant what specific courses I still need to take to complete my degree, so that I can ensure I am on track to graduate without spending hours deciphering the course catalog and my student portal.
- As a student seeking guidance, I want to tell the AI assistant to find my academic advisor's next available appointment, so that I can easily book a meeting without having to log into a separate advising or calendar system.
- As a career-focused student, I want the AI assistant to proactively recommend relevant studio events, workshops, and networking opportunities based on my major and interests, so that I can dedicate my time to building my craft and career rather than hunting through scattered campus bulletin boards and newsletters.

2. AI Assignment Planner 
- As a student facing a large, open-ended project, I want to upload or paste my assignment brief into the application, so that the AI can analyze it and suggest a step-by-step, actionable workflow (e.g., Research, Outline, First Draft, Revisions).
- As a student with limited free time, I want the application to integrate directly with my digital calendar (e.g., Google Calendar, Apple Calendar), so that the AI can automatically schedule my broken-down project milestones around my existing classes, work shifts, and other commitments.
- As a student struggling to start an intimidating assignment, I want the AI to prompt me with strategic brainstorming questions and low-stakes "first steps," so that I can build momentum and begin the creative process rather than staring at a blank screen.

3. AI Professor/Assignement Topic Bot
Student Experience
- As a student, I want to query a course-specific "topic bot" at any time of day so that I can get instant, accurate answers to my questions while working on assignments outside of regular office hours.
- As a student, I want assurance that the AI's responses are generated strictly from my professors' verified materials so that I avoid hallucinated or generic web information that contradicts my specific Columbia College program guidelines.
- As a student, I want to seamlessly switch between different topic bots (e.g., a Graphic Design bot vs. an Audio Arts bot) so that I receive contextualized answers relevant to the exact major or course sequence I am currently studying.
- As a student, I want the AI to provide citations or links back to the original syllabus, lecture slide, or required reading so that I can independently verify the context and dive deeper into the material.
Faculty Experience
- As a professor, I want to upload and curate my course materials into a shared repository with other faculty so that we can collaboratively build a unified "canon" of knowledge for our department.
- As a professor, I want the web application to automatically answer repetitive logistical and syllabus-based questions so that I can dedicate my email time and office hours to higher-level mentoring and creative problem-solving.
- As a professor, I want to view a weekly dashboard of the most frequently asked questions by my students so that I can easily identify bottlenecks in comprehension or confusing assignment instructions.
- As a professor, I want the ability to update, correct, or refine the approved knowledge base mid-semester so that the bot's understanding naturally evolves alongside classroom discussions and industry shifts.
- As a professor, I want to set parameters or "exploration pathways" for the topic bot so that the AI guides students through a deliberate learning process rather than just giving them the final answer.
Department & Course Design
- As a department chair or curriculum designer, I want to analyze aggregated trends in student inquiries across an entire course sequence so that I can evaluate whether the program's overarching learning goals are being met and adjust future curricula accordingly.
